[Music]  the 2021 state of devops report released  last year  it contained findings about how teams  navigate key areas of software delivery  we talked with two of the researchers  dustin smith and dave stanky about the  reports section on security  hi dustin and dave were there any  surprising findings in this section of  the report you would think that the more  steps i add into my process the slower  it will be but i think it's really about  how teams are integrating these  practices and are they doing it by  looking at their entire system and  figuring out how they can best fit them  into the actual process so that it is  seamless and that should be the idea  with any of these practices  and we find that teams that implement  these practices are actually delivering  software faster and more reliability and  all while seeing value driven to the  business so one thing that we found  consistently and yet i'm still surprised  by it every time is that we don't have  to make a compromise or a trade-off  between delivering fast and delivering  well we found that teams that have the  best secure code practices actually are  the same ones that are delivering the  most frequently and the having the  shortest lead time from code to delivery  so we really can have both fast and and  and  market responsiveness and security and  reliability  what exactly did the security section of  the report focus on  i think one of the important things to  clarify  with how we looked at security is that  we weren't looking at things like how  many vulnerabilities did you experience  in the last year we weren't measuring  how secure is your team  instead what we focused on were what  practices have you implemented on your  team in order to detect and recover from  security vulnerabilities  in  an effective way  that's one of the key differences that  we try to focus on  for several reasons  one of the main ones is that  people may not be willing to share how  many vulnerabilities they've experienced  but they are willing to share what  practices they've built into their  process  what did you find separated elite teams  from the rest  the real fundamental thing that we see  is they do something called shifting  left on security the teams that are  succeeding are the ones that are  bringing security principles knowledge  and practices leftward into their  testing into their initial development  processes and making it something that  is uh known and and practiced throughout  the organization  they make a lot of use of automated  testing to to make it so that known  vulnerabilities can rapidly be detected  and and kicked back for remediation  and something that really high  performing teams like to do is having  guardrails or or pre-approved libraries  things that developers can pull into  their code with the assurance that those  have been vetted by experts and that  they're going to be  going to pass their security tests  because they've already been  built in the security practices have  already been built in elite performing  teams are more likely to build security  into their entire development process  and so that means not throwing over your  most recent change to the security team  and asking for approval right before  deployment and hoping that they do it  quickly it means pulling them  in earlier right at the designed dock  phase are you including your security  team  and then are you including security  reviews throughout your entire process  as well  and then do you have tests that check  for known vulnerabilities built into  your build process  and finally do you have pre-approved  libraries that you can pull into your  project and know that those  versions of those dependencies are  pre-approved and  secure  and ready to be used  how can teams start implementing these  practices  let's say go to the report  and look at all the practices that we've  outlined and try to identify some of  those quick wins that you can get  where's the where are the low-hanging  fruit and one of the nice things about  the report is that we also include  industry benchmarks for each practice so  you can get a rough idea of to what  degree has this practice already been  adopted in the industry  that should give you an idea even if you  haven't met with your team yet of where  potentially are we lacking  what would you say is the core idea from  this section of the report  really the meme is security is  everybody's job  um but that doesn't mean that it's not  your job it's uh it can be an  unfortunate interpretation that says  well if it's everybody's job then then  i'm covered i don't need to worry about  it right  well the the bad news is yeah it's your  job too and we find that teams that  succeed are the ones where everyone in  the organization really  internalizes the importance of shipping  secure code  now that doesn't mean that we expect  every person in an organization to be a  security expert and  a worldwide leader in in in software  security instead what we like to see is  a consultative model where an  organization has a team of real security  experts who are creating guidelines  creating uh pre-approved pipelines and  generally educating the organization  about how to incorporate security into  their work and then everyone throughout  the organization from design to  implementation to testing to operations  all can reflect that knowledge back into  the work so that it's re so that it's  incorporated in their day-to-day jobs i  think the over  arching principle should be that  we need to move from being reactive to  detective vulnerabilities and more  proactive and even diagnostic and just  noting that vulnerabilities are  essentially inevitable and so we need to  be able to come up with practices and  processes in order to recover from them  when they are detected in a way that is  quick  and scalable  where can users go to learn more  we've got a great resource on our google  cloud site that incorporates this year's  state of devops report as well as  previous years it's got a capability  catalog that shows all those  capabilities i mentioned and for each  one there's an article that talks about  what it is how to measure it and how to  get started practicing it  there's all that and more including a  self-assessment tool that i really  recommend you can check it all out at g  dot co  devops  [Music]  you

